1. Twisted Pair Cable
What is Twisted Pair Cable?

Twisted Pair Cable consists of pairs of copper wires twisted together.

The twisting helps reduce:

Electromagnetic interference (EMI)
Signal loss
Noise
Structure

A twisted pair cable contains:

Plastic Cover

     |
     |
----------------
|  Copper Wire |
|  Copper Wire |
----------------


The wires are twisted around each other.

Why Are Wires Twisted?

Imagine two people talking in a noisy room.

If they are close, outside noise affects communication.

Similarly, electrical signals can be disturbed by nearby devices.

Twisting reduces interference.

Types of Twisted Pair Cable

There are two main types:

1. UTP (Unshielded Twisted Pair)

No additional protection.

Example:

Home Ethernet cables
Office networks

Advantages:

Cheap
Lightweight
Easy installation

Disadvantages:

More affected by interference
2. STP (Shielded Twisted Pair)

Has an additional metallic shield.

Structure:

Outer Cover

↓

Metal Shield

↓

Twisted Copper Wires

Advantages:

Better protection from interference
More reliable

Disadvantages:

Expensive
Heavier
